<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Pontano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1426">1426</a>–<a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni Gioviano Pontano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Pontano (1426–1503) was an Italian humanist, poet and statesman. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, he served the Aragonese kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for decades as secretary and chief minister under Ferrante. He wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry, dialogues and treatises on politics and astronomy, and he led the Neapolitan academy of humanists, which took his name as the Accademia Pontaniana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-pontano.jpg" alt="Giovanni Pontano" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adriano_fiorentino,_busto_di_giovanni_pontano,_1490_ca._(genova,_museo_di_sant%27agostino)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfonso V made Naples a centre of Renaissance humanism, patronising scholars such as Lorenzo Valla and Antonio Beccadelli, whose circle grew into the academy later named after <b>Giovanni Pontano</b>, and the triumphal arch of the Castel Nuovo is among the earliest Renaissance monuments of southern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cerreto di Spoleto, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.82,12.92&amp;year=1476" aria-label="Cerreto di Spoleto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 642 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giovanni Pontano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.82,12.92&amp;year=1476">What was here in 1476? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
